1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,422
An investigation can find out why something failed.

2
00:00:03,422 --> 00:00:06,511
But what happens when the same mistake happens again?

3
00:00:06,511 --> 00:00:13,155
Most incident reports are read once, then filed away, and the lesson stays trapped inside the report it came from.

4
00:00:13,155 --> 00:00:15,195
AFTERMATH exists to close that gap.

5
00:00:15,225 --> 00:00:22,377
This is the real, deployed product, and it is public: a visitor submits failure material with no account and no approval.

6
00:00:22,377 --> 00:00:24,209
I will use the built-in sample.

7
00:00:24,209 --> 00:00:32,306
A checkout service returned a 503, the charge status came back unknown, and an agent still reported the charge as completed successfully.

8
00:00:32,306 --> 00:00:39,931
AFTERMATH parses the material strictly as data, never executes it, and grounds every fact in a verbatim span from the submission.

9
00:00:39,969 --> 00:00:47,355
The result is a Safeguard Record: evidence with its verbatim spans, a hypothesis with a confidence, and one proposed safeguard.

10
00:00:47,355 --> 00:00:49,566
Notice the evidence status is Unknown.

11
00:00:49,566 --> 00:00:55,614
The material names no authoritative source, so AFTERMATH refuses to decide whether the charge succeeded.

12
00:00:55,614 --> 00:00:58,348
That refusal is the product working, not a gap.

13
00:00:58,355 --> 00:01:05,401
Verified safeguards are written to Walrus Memory on Mainnet, and a write counts as verified only when a recall query returns it.

14
00:01:05,401 --> 00:01:16,025
This ledger reads live from the relayer: one hundred eighty completed writes, one hundred seventy-seven recall-verified, zero simulated, and three hundred thirty-five memories in the namespace.

15
00:01:16,037 --> 00:01:17,822
Here is the moment that matters.

16
00:01:17,822 --> 00:01:23,008
A later investigation reports a build passing in continuous integration with no log attached.

17
00:01:23,008 --> 00:01:31,317
AFTERMATH recalls an applicable safeguard, then runs the decision engine twice over identical evidence: once with the memory held out, once enforced.

18
00:01:31,317 --> 00:01:33,213
The only difference is the memory.

19
00:01:33,213 --> 00:01:39,794
The verdict is unchanged, but the memory adds two required verification checks the engine would not otherwise require.

20
00:01:39,794 --> 00:01:45,259
Provenance shows the chain from that incident, to this Walrus blob, to these later investigations.

21
00:01:45,284 --> 00:01:48,361
AFTERMATH does not merely remember that something happened.

22
00:01:48,361 --> 00:01:52,271
It makes the next investigation check what the last one taught us to check.

23
00:01:52,271 --> 00:01:54,305
Every failure should leave a safeguard.

